  <ym-title|Equation-adapted microscopic section><label|equation-adapted-microscopic-section>

  New fragment: chapters/02-equation-section.tex. Insert after the
  small-field bounds in Chapter 2, before Examples and the remaining
  normalization problem. It uses no Chapter 3 theorem; its solution-jet ideal
  agrees with ker omega_d subsequently defined there.

  Source checked in full: Martin/existence/marked-campaign-producer.tex,
  lines 1\U631. Main joins:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>120\U185: safe-edge curvature coordinates span finite radial jet
    dual.

    <item>185\U235: homogeneous equation ideal factorization.

    <item>235\U320: forces span the TOP-layer equation annihilators. The
    fragment deliberately does not assert that every layer of a
    discrete-force-null jet is a continuum solution.

    <item>320\U393: spectators represented with coordinates avoiding the
    force edge on the full degree-d jet space; strict lexicographic
    factor/weight error.

    <item>393\U430: one-link Haar integration with arbitrary bounded exterior
    spectators; period threshold prevents aliasing of distinguished edge.

    <item>430\U631: gauge and geometric averaging, finite grading
    triangularity, exact section V=F R^{-1}.
  </itemize-minus>

  Original standard ingredients: Wilson action (Wilson1974), compact-group
  lattice gauge integration (OsterwalderSeiler1978), elementary
  finite-dimensional annihilator duality and Haar integration. The particular
  safe-edge triangular construction is proved, not attributed to an
  unverified published theorem.

  No RG calibration or all-period continuum conclusion is asserted here.
  Exact finite-period equation identity holds uniformly above a fixed stencil
  period threshold, for every nonzero real beta. The local small-field
  derivative estimate follows from the already proved
  powered-curvature-factor section estimate. The old Chapter 2 closing prose
  should be adjusted when integrating: it can now state that raw equation
  representatives are available but RG normalization is still required.
</body>

